# {{lb|en|legal}} [[Legislative]] action for the purpose of [[regulating]] the use of property and the construction of buildings within the area under the [[jurisdiction]] of the legislative body concerned.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Oliver Wainwright|title=Super-tall, super-skinny, super-expensive: the ‘pencil towers’ of New York’s super-rich|editor=w:Katharine Viner|newspaper=w:The Guardian|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20201005122034/https://www.theguardian.com/cities/2019/feb/05/super-tall-super-skinny-super-expensive-the-pencil-towers-of-new-yorks-super-rich|archivedate=5 October 2020|location=London|publisher=[[w:Guardian Media Group|Guardian News & Media]]|date=1 June 2019|issn=0261-3077|oclc=229952407|passage=These towers are not only the product of advances in construction technology&nbsp;– and a global surfeit of super-rich buyers&nbsp;– but a '''zoning''' policy that allows a developer to acquire unused airspace nearby, add it to their own lot, and erect a vast structure without any kind of public review process taking place.}}
# The use of zones, such as fare zones.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Andrew Martin|title=Underground Overground: A Passenger’s History of the Tube|publisher=Profile Books|page=244|isbn=9781846684777|passage=The system of '''zoning''' would lead logically to the introduction of the modern [[ticket barrier]]s from 1987 (the [[Underground]] Ticketing System, or UTS), in that it allowed the software in the ticket gates to be relatively simple.}}

